{
  "slug": "artist:yellowman",
  "name": "Yellowman",
  "editorial_article": "Winston Foster, better known as Yellowman or King Yellowman, rose from Kingston's sound-system scene in the early 1980s as a pivotal dancehall deejay. After finishing second in the 1978 Tastee Talent Contest, he won the competition the following year, which launched his professional career. He is widely credited with helping define the coarse, direct sound of modern dancehall. In 1981, he became the first dancehall artist signed to a major American label, Columbia Records. Throughout the early 1980s, he maintained a prolific output, frequently collaborating with producer Henry 'Junjo' Lawes and performing alongside deejay Fathead. His 1982 track 'Zungguzungguguzungguzeng', built on the 'Diseases' riddim, became a cornerstone of dancehall culture and has been sampled extensively across reggae and hip-hop. Despite undergoing invasive jaw surgery for cancer in the mid-1980s, he returned to the stage by 1987. His career includes two Grammy Award nominations for Best Reggae Album and a 2018 Order of Distinction from the Jamaican government.",
